  "prediction": "**Tennis and Sports News**\n\nIn recent developments, tennis superstar Serena Williams has announced her return to training after a prolonged absence due to injury and illness. The 13-time Grand Slam champion, who has been ranked No. 10 in the latest rankings, has been taking it one day at a time and is not setting a specific timeline for her comeback. Williams had been on blood thinners for blood clots and had undergone an emergency procedure for a pulmonary embolism in February. Her agent, Jill Smoller, stated that Williams will return to the WTA Tour when she feels strong and healthy, in consultation with her doctors.\n\nMeanwhile, world No. 2 Kim Clijsters has suffered a freak foot accident that may keep her out of the French Open. Clijsters sustained a severe strain of her medial and lateral ligaments in her right ankle and torn ligaments in her ankle joint, and is expected to be out for at least four to six weeks.\n\nIn other tennis news, Fort Lauderdale's Ryan Sweeting has won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori 6-4, 7-6 (7-3) in the U.S. Clay Court Championship in Houston. Sweeting, who was born in the Bahamas and moved to Fort Lauderdale at 12, has moved up from No. 93 to No. 71 in the rankings.\n\nThe U.S. Fed Cup team will rely on 18-year-old Christina McHale and 19-year-old Melanie Oudin in a world group playoff against Germany this weekend. The teenagers, who train at the USTA training facility in Boca Raton, are expected to face a tough challenge from the German team.\n\n**Internet Archive News**\n\nIn a separate development, Alexa Internet has been donating its crawl data to the Internet Archive since 1996. The data, which flow in every day, are added to the Wayback Machine after an embargo period. This donation has helped to create a vast archive of the internet, allowing users to access historical versions of websites and web pages.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Serena Williams has returned to training after a prolonged absence due to injury and illness.\n* Kim Clijsters may miss the French Open due to a freak foot accident.\n* Ryan Sweeting has won his first ATP title, defeating Kei Nishikori in the U.S. Clay Court Championship.\n* The U.S. Fed Cup team will rely on 18-year-old Christina McHale and 19-year-old Melanie Oudin",
